125:  * <h3>Accessing Guice bound objects in Handlers</h3>
126:  * <p>
127:  * There are two ways to use Guice bound objects in your handler implementations.
128:  * </p>
129:  * <h4>Dependency Injected Handlers</h4>
130:  * <p>
131:  * The {@code handler()} methods used to create a Guice backed application take an {@link Action} that operates on a {@link Chain} instance.
132:  * This chain instance given to this action provides a Guice backed {@link Registry} via its {@link Chain#getRegistry()} method.
133:  * This registry is able to retrieve objects that were explicitly bound (i.e. defined by a module), and bind objects “just in time”.
134:  * This means that it can be used to construct dependency injected {@link Handler} implementations.
135:  * </p>
136:  * <p>
137:  * Simply pass the class of the handler implementation to create a new dependency injected instance of to this method,
138:  * then add it to the chain.
139:  * </p>
140:  * <p>
141:  * See the code above for an example of this.
142:  * </p>
143:  * <h4>Accessing dependencies via context registry lookup</h4>
144:  * <p>
145:  * The {@link ratpack.handling.Context} object that is given to a handler's {@link Handler#handle(ratpack.handling.Context)}
146:  * method is also a registry implementation. In a Guice backed app, Guice bound objects can be retrieved via the {@link Registry#get(Class)} method of
